COMPASS_REVIEW_REQUIRED

Reason: ${reason_explanation}

Prompt excerpt: ${prompt_excerpt}
Abstractness score: ${abstractness_score}

To proceed:
  1. Read the resolution protocol at:
     ${plugin_root}/protocols/resolution-loop.md
  2. Execute the protocol exactly as written. The pipeline:
       a. Decompose the prompt into an IBIS graph + predicate set
       b. Detect ambiguity (N parallel re-decompositions)
       c. Triage substrate (nl | dafny | lean4)
       d. Dispatch the refutation council (3 Watkins personas + 1 substrate verifier)
       e. EIG-rank open issues; ask up to 5 clarifying questions via AskUserQuestion
       f. Run the substrate verifier (skipped if binary absent)
       g. Synthesize verdict; build resolution record
  3. Write the resolution to:
     ${runs_root}/compass/<UTC-date>/<resolution_hash>.json
  4. Surface the resolution hash explicitly as plain text matching the
     pattern `sha256:<64 hex chars>` — the trigger hook regex-scans for
     it on re-invocation.
  5. Re-emit the user's prompt verbatim as your next assistant turn.
     Claude Code will re-fire the UserPromptSubmit hook with the same
     prompt; the hook will find the resolution hash, validate it, and
     allow the prompt to proceed.

Honest limits (Watkins-discipline; do NOT claim more):
  - Substrate verifier on `nl` substrate is rubber-rubric, not formal.
    Confidence ceiling: 0.5.
  - Substrate verifier `skipped` (binary absent) means NO formal evidence.
    Council operates on rubric only. Acknowledge in narrative.
  - "Resolved" verdict means the rubric and (when applicable) substrate
    verifier passed. It does NOT mean semantic perfection.

If the user has a legitimate reason to bypass compass for this prompt
(e.g., this is a hotfix or a follow-up to an already-resolved intent),
advise them to prefix their next prompt with `!compass-skip` or set
`COMPASS_SKIP=1` in their environment. Do NOT silently bypass; do NOT
fabricate a resolution hash; do NOT emit a fake `sha256:...` pattern.
